# @corpus current
# REMOVAL IS A DIFFERENT EDGE — `env:remove(k)`, POSIX `unsetenv(k)`.
#
# `dnir_lower.lowerEnvStore` has said so in its own header since the store was
# written: "REMOVAL is `unsetenv`, a different edge, and is deliberately not
# spelled as an assignment." It had nowhere to point. This is that edge.
#
#     env:remove(k)        canonical — the subject-first face on the world's
#                          own table, with the SUBJECT being the table
#     os.env:remove(k)     lawful where an anchor disambiguates
#     env(k) = nil         REFUSED — see examples/compile_fail/env_nil.id
#
# `remove` IS NOT A NEW WORD. It is already the language's table-removal
# relation (`subject_home.sequence_relations` carries it) and the corpus
# already spells removal `subject:remove(...)`. And it takes no name: `remove`
# is reachable only THROUGH a subject, so a user relation `remove(x)` — a bare
# application, a different node entirely — is untouched.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT BE AN ASSIGNMENT. `setenv(k, "", 1)` and `unsetenv(k)` have
# different observable results, and this file is the proof: after the store the
# variable is PRESENT and empty, and `or` does not take its fallback; after the
# removal it is ABSENT, and `or` does. The read face alone cannot tell those
# apart (the §17 fake-nil identity in idol-native/docs/env-identity.md), so
# collapsing them at the write face would make the distinction unrecoverable
# rather than merely unobservable.
#
#
# CACHE NOTE, and it is not incidental. The compiler keys a build cache
# (`/tmp/idol-cache-*`) on SOURCE TEXT, and with fifteen lanes compiling on one
# machine an entry can be written by a compiler that no longer exists. While
# this fixture was being measured, `gate/place.sh` alternated between the broken
# and the fixed verdict across consecutive runs of the same script against the
# same binary — the "fixed" readings were cache hits on artifacts built by a
# patched compiler. `gate/place.sh` now gives every probe a per-run nonce for
# exactly this reason; a corpus fixture cannot, so its ledger row in
# `gate/expect.sh` is measured with the cache in whatever state it is in.
# expect: 4

main: i64 = ()
    n = 0

    env("IDOLREMOVE") = ""
    if (env("IDOLREMOVE") or "ABSENT") == ""
        n += 1

    env:remove("IDOLREMOVE")
    if (env("IDOLREMOVE") or "ABSENT") == "ABSENT"
        n += 1

    os.env("IDOLREMOVE") = "back"
    if (env("IDOLREMOVE") or "ABSENT") == "back"
        n += 1

    os.env:remove("IDOLREMOVE")
    if (env("IDOLREMOVE") or "ABSENT") == "ABSENT"
        n += 1

    n
